.TH UL80 1 "November 2025" "um80 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ul80 \- Microsoft LINK-80 compatible linker for 8080/Z80
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ul80
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput.com\fR]
[\fB\-x\fR]
[\fB\-\-prl\fR]
[\fB\-\-allow\-overlap\fR]
[\fB\-\-fatal\-mult\-def\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR]
[\fB\-S\fR \fIfile.sym\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIorigin\fR]
.I file.rel
[\fIfile.rel\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ul80
is a linker that is compatible with Microsoft LINK-80.
.B ul80
combines the REL files that
.BR um80 (1)
writes into CP/M COM files.
.PP
The linker resolves external symbols.
The linker places the code, the data and the COMMON blocks in memory.
The linker writes a binary program that can run under CP/M or in a CP/M
emulator.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-version
Write the version information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " output.com"
Name of the output file.
The default is the name of the first input file, with the extension .com.
.TP
.B \-x
Write a HEX file (Intel HEX format) instead of a binary file.
The HEX file is useful for EPROM programmers.
.TP
.B \-\-prl
Write an MP/M PRL file (Page Relocatable format) instead of a COM file.
MP/M can load a PRL file at any page boundary in memory.
The PRL format is useful in the multi-user environment of MP/M.
The output file has a 256-byte header with the code size and the BSS size,
the code section, and a relocation bitmap.
The relocation bitmap marks the bytes that need page adjustment when the
program loads at a different address.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-overlap
Link absolute code that loads over other code, with a warning, as LINK-80
does.
A patch module and an overlay module are examples of such code.
Without this option, such code is an error.
The image has the byte that ul80 loads last:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
When two modules load a byte at one address, the byte of the later module
is in the image.
.IP \(bu 2
When one module loads a byte at one address more than one time, the byte
that the module loads later is in the image.
.RE
.IP
ul80 writes the image as LINK-80 writes it when LINK-80 has the option /D.
.TP
.B \-\-fatal\-mult\-def
Make a public symbol that more than one module defines an error.
Then ul80 writes no output and sets the exit status to 1.
Without this option, such a public symbol is a warning, as in LINK-80
("%Mult. Def. Global").
Then every reference uses the first definition that ul80 loads, and ul80
writes the output.
In both cases, ul80 names both modules:
.RS
.nf
Warning: %Mult. Def. Global FOO: defined in A and in B; A's definition is used
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B \-s
Write a symbol file (.SYM).
The name of the symbol file is based on the name of the output file.
ul80 makes this name.
The symbol file has all PUBLIC symbols and their final relocated addresses.
.TP
.BI \-S " file.sym"
Write a symbol file with the name that you give.
To write a complete symbol file with all labels, use this option with the
option \fB\-g\fR of the assembler:
.RS
.nf
um80 \-g program.mac
ul80 \-S program.sym program.rel
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI \-d " addr" ", " \-\-data\-origin " addr"
Place DSEG at \fIaddr\fR (hexadecimal) and COMMON after DSEG (LINK-80 /D:).
.TP
.BI \-\-common\-origin " addr"
Place COMMON (BSS) at \fIaddr\fR (hexadecimal).
.TP
.B \-\-rom\-data
Store the initial bytes of DSEG in the image, after the code.
The DSEG symbols keep their RAM addresses.
This option needs \fB\-d\fR.
ul80 defines __DATA_ROM, __DATA_RAM, __DATA_LEN and __DATA_END.
With \fB\-x\fR, ul80 writes each sequence of consecutive bytes at its load
address and skips the gaps.
.TP
.B \-\-equ\-file \fIfile\fR
Write every public symbol as NAME EQU 0xxxxH, for a bank module to INCLUDE.
.TP
.B \-\-bin
Write a BIN file: a raw image from the origin, with no 128-byte CP/M padding.
.TP
.BI \-\-fill " byte"
Fill byte (hexadecimal) for the gaps and the padding with \fB\-\-bin\fR
(default FF).
.TP
.BI \-\-rom\-size " n"
ROM size (hexadecimal).
The option \fB\-\-bin\fR pads the image to \fIn\fR bytes.
A larger image is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-ram\-end " addr" ", " \-\-ram\-size " n"
End of RAM (the first address after RAM) or size of RAM, in hexadecimal.
Data and BSS past the end of RAM are an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-stack " n"
Bytes (hexadecimal) that must remain free in RAM after BSS.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Write a one-line summary of ROM usage and RAM usage.
.TP
.BI \-p " origin"
Set the origin of the program.
The default is 100 (hexadecimal).
The address 100 is the standard load address of a CP/M transient program.
The value is hexadecimal by default, for example E000.
The value can have a prefix: 0x for hexadecimal, 0o for octal.
.SH MEMORY LAYOUT
The linker arranges memory as follows:
.IP 1. 3
The code segment (CSEG) starts at the origin address.
The code of each module starts after the code of the module before it.
.IP 2.
The data segment (DSEG) starts immediately after the code.
.IP 3.
The COMMON blocks start after the data.
.PP
ul80 loads absolute code (ASEG) at its own address.
When the highest absolute location that the earlier modules reached is above
where the code of a module would go, the code of the module starts above that
location.
LINK-80 places the code in the same way.
For example, after
.B ASEG
/
.B ORG 100H
in one module, the code of the next module starts after that absolute code.
.PP
Absolute code is an error in these cases, and ul80 writes no output:
.IP \(bu 2
The absolute code overlaps the code of a module.
.IP \(bu 2
The absolute code overlaps the data or COMMON.
.IP \(bu 2
The absolute code overlaps the absolute code of another module.
.PP
With \fB\-\-allow\-overlap\fR, each of these cases is a warning.
.PP
When LINK-80 does not have the option /P, LINK-80 starts at 0103H.
The address 0103H leaves room for a JMP at 0100H in absolute code.
ul80 starts at the origin, 0100H by default.
To link such a program, use \fB\-p 103\fR.
.SH LINKING PROCESS
The linker does these steps:
.IP 1. 3
Load all input REL files
.IP 2.
Resolve external symbols between modules
.IP 3.
Give final addresses to all segments
.IP 4.
Apply the address fixups for relocatable references
.IP 5.
Write the output file
.PP
If the linker does not find an external symbol in any loaded module, the
linker reports an error.
The linker searches for undefined symbols in the library files (.LIB) that
the command line names.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Link a single module:
.B ul80 program.rel
.TP
Link multiple modules:
.B ul80 \-o app.com main.rel io.rel util.rel
.TP
Write a symbol file:
.B ul80 \-s program.rel
.TP
Set a custom origin (hexadecimal with no prefix):
.B ul80 \-p E000 program.rel
.TP
Write a symbol file with a name:
.B ul80 \-S program.sym program.rel
.TP
Write a HEX file:
.B ul80 \-x \-o program.hex program.rel
.TP
Write a PRL file:
.B ul80 \-\-prl program.rel
.TP
Link with a library file:
.B ul80 \-o program.com main.rel mylib.lib
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The link step succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The link step failed because of errors.
Examples of such errors are undefined symbols, absolute code that overlaps
other code, and a public symbol that more than one module defines
(with \fB\-\-fatal\-mult\-def\fR).
Other errors also give this exit status.
ul80 writes no output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I input.rel
REL file (from um80).
.TP
.I input.lib
Library file (from ulib80).
.TP
.I output.com
COM file: the image from the origin, or from the lowest absolute address if
absolute code is at an address below the origin.
.TP
.I output.hex
HEX file, in Intel HEX format (with the option \-x).
.TP
.I output.prl
PRL file, in MP/M Page Relocatable format (with the option \-\-prl).
.TP
.I output.sym
Symbol file (with the option \-s).
.SH SYMBOL FILE FORMAT
The symbol file has one line for each symbol:
.PP
.RS
.nf
SYMBOL_NAME  XXXX
.fi
.RE
.PP
XXXX is the 16-bit address of the symbol, in hexadecimal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR um80 (1),
.BR ulib80 (1),
.BR ucref80 (1),
.BR ud80 (1)
.PP
The original Microsoft LINK-80 manual is in the retro_docs archive,
https://github.com/avwohl/retro_docs/blob/main/um80_and_friends/l80.pdf
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B ul80
is compatible with Microsoft LINK-80 (L80).
The REL format is binary-compatible.
Thus ul80 can link REL files that M80 writes, and L80 can link REL files
that um80 writes.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
ul80 searches the libraries in the order that the command line gives.
ul80 searches the modules of each library in library order.
LINK-80 does the same.
.IP \(bu 2
ul80 takes from a library only the modules that have symbols that satisfy
undefined references.
A library can be one that ulib80 made or one that LIB-80 made
(REL modules one after another).
.IP \(bu 2
ul80 loads every module of a REL file that has more than one module.
.IP \(bu 2
COMMON blocks that have the same name in different modules share storage.
The block gets the largest size that a module requests.
ul80 places each named block separately, after the data, in the order in
which the modules first declare the blocks.
The bytes that a module assembles into a COMMON block are loaded in that
block.
.IP \(bu 2
A chain is the list of references to an external symbol.
A chain can also be a FORTRAN-80 forward reference that the special link item 12
closes.
ul80 follows a chain through every word.
For each chain entry, ul80 uses the type of the chain entry.
An absolute chain entry is an address in ASEG.
In a REL file from um80 0.3.48 or earlier, the rule is different.
There, an absolute chain entry is an offset in the segment of the word that
holds the chain entry.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 0.2.0 to 0.3.34 chained the references to an external symbol through
such untyped chain entries across segments.
ul80 cannot follow such chain entries where the chain entries cross segments.
Assemble such a REL file again.
.IP \(bu 2
If a module defines the public symbol $MEMRY, ul80 sets the word at that
symbol to the first free address after the data and COMMON, as LINK-80 sets
the word.
The library of FORTRAN-80 allocates from that word.
.IP \(bu 2
The layout of ul80 is code, then data, then COMMON.
This layout is the layout of LINK-80 with /D: at the end of the code, except
that LINK-80 puts COMMON at the start of the data area.
.IP \(bu 2
Without /D, LINK-80 loads the data of each module before the code of the
module.
ul80 does not.
.IP \(bu 2
ul80 starts the code of a module above the absolute code that the earlier
modules loaded.
LINK-80 does the same without /D.
LINK-80 with /D does not.
.IP \(bu 2
LINK-80 writes a COM file from 0100H, whatever /P says.
After a set-location item to ASEG 0000H, LINK-80 writes the COM file from
0000H.
ul80 writes the COM file from the origin.
See docs/EXTENSIONS.md.
.IP \(bu 2
A link-time expression is an extension link item in a REL file.
MACRO-80 3.44 and um80 write these items for HIGH or LOW of relocatable or
external values, and for similar expressions.
ul80 evaluates the expressions after it places all modules.
.IP \(bu 2
In a PRL file or an SPR file, ul80 marks a byte that is HIGH of an address
in the relocation bitmap.
ul80 does not mark a byte that is LOW of an address.
If an expression would not move by exactly 0 or 1 page, the expression is an
error.
.SH AUTHOR
um80 project contributors.
